# ============================================================================
# TWO-STEP PROBE (plans/cycle-1-craft-the-scenario/01-author.md)
# ROUND 5 on coolify. RE-QUESTIONED from measured runs. Anchor unchanged: Server.
#
# THE ONE VARIABLE THIS DRAFT MOVES: THE GOLD GROUP. NOT THE WORDING.
#   Both steps below are byte-for-byte the two-step probe that measured baseline 0.077 /
#   sense 0.846 (scenarios/coolify.yaml.probe-two-step.bak, diffed this session). What
#   changes is `dependents`: four rows out, four rows in, on the pooled per-row citation
#   ranking quoted below. Wording and gold are not moved together, because a cell that
#   moves both cannot say which one paid.
#
# WHICH SIDE OF THE WINDOW I AM CORRECTING FROM, AND HOW FAR
#   The window is baseline <= 0.50 AND delta >= +0.50, both at once.
#   The last measured cell fell on the BASELINE side: the seven-step expansion of this
#   probe scored baseline 9 of 13 = 0.692, sense 13 of 13 = 1.000, delta +0.308
#   (results/claude-opus-5/validation/ef2de675520f658c, `credit_table.py coolify`, run this
#   session). Our arm is AT the ceiling and cannot move up, so the entire correction is
#   baseline-side and it has to be worth at least 0.192 of recall in session.
#
# THE TRAJECTORY, EVERY CYCLE, AND WHICH SIDE EACH FELL ON
#   Read off the `# The two numbers` section of each minibench/validate report on disk
#   (results/dryrun/coolify/minibench.1..19.md, results/loop/coolify/minibench.md and
#   validate.md). baseline / sense on `dependents`; side = which half of the window failed.
#     a1  0.944 / 1.000  baseline side      a11 0.800 / 0.933  baseline side
#     a2  0.846 / 0.846  baseline side      a12 0.929 / 1.000  baseline side
#     a3  0.357 / 0.571  sense side         a13 0.786 / 0.786  baseline side
#     a4  0.929 / 0.571  baseline side      a14 0.267 / 0.267  sense side
#     a5  0.000 / 0.333  sense side         a15 0.333 / 0.200  sense side
#     a6  VOID  / 0.917  no baseline arm    a16 0.200 / 0.467  sense side
#     a7  0.750 / 0.917  baseline side      a17 0.533 / 0.867  baseline side
#     a8  0.000 / 0.750  sense-arm defect   a18 0.308 / 1.000  INSIDE (probe)
#     a9  0.000 / 0.250  sense side          +  0.538 / 0.615  baseline side (7-step)
#     a10 0.917 / 0.833  baseline side      a19 0.571 / 0.429  baseline side
#                                           a20 0.077 / 0.846  INSIDE (probe)
#                                            +  0.692 / 1.000  baseline side (7-step)
#
#   THE SPLIT THAT MATTERS, AND IT IS NOT THE WORDING. a18 and a20 ran THIS step 2 and
#   THESE thirteen gold rows in a two-step session and put the baseline at 0.308 and 0.077.
#   The seven-step expansions of the same two runs, with step 2 moved to index 4 unchanged,
#   put it at 0.538 and 0.692. Same words, same gold, +0.35 on average from the session
#   alone. Per-unit, both two-step baselines sit below both seven-step baselines - there is
#   no overlap - so re-wording step 2 is aiming at a variable that has already been held
#   constant across the split. n = 2 per side; it is a two-point split, not a law.
#
# THE SUPERSEDED QUESTION AND THE NUMBER THAT KILLED IT
#   scenarios/coolify.yaml.seven-step-baseline-0692.bak - the seven-step session expanded
#   from a20's probe. baseline 0.692, sense 1.000, delta +0.308, verdict SCENARIO. The nine
#   rows it took: d:additional-network-pivot, d:ca-cert-job-id, d:cloud-provider-external-id,
#   d:cloud-status-lock-key, d:global-search-state-id, d:new-destination-state-id,
#   d:new-resource-state-id, d:proxy-config-state-id, d:sentinel-event-uuid. Its arm ran
#   488 of 488 seconds to `hard_cap_timeout` (run_meta.json, quoted), so 0.692 is a
#   CENSORED-LOW number and the real in-session figure is at least that.
#
# THE RE-GOLD, AND THE RANKING IT COMES FROM
#   Pooled per-row citation over every run on disk carrying this identical thirteen-row id
#   set - shas 55ee5f19fe618a13, 642901f8911cd688, 5d03634c2fe30242, e733f4d2fda1787b,
#   ef2de675520f658c; 4 baseline runs and 5 sense runs, all claude-opus-5, id sets verified
#   identical. This is the first point in the campaign at or near the ~5-runs-per-arm floor
#   the ranking law requires, which is why a re-gold is made now and was not made before.
#
#     row                            baseline   sense
#     d:logs-ownership-check           0/4       4/5    KEPT - the one perfect discriminator
#     d:container-webhook-handle       1/4       2/5    KEPT
#     d:new-destination-state-id       1/4       4/5    KEPT
#     d:new-resource-state-id          1/4       5/5    KEPT
#     d:additional-network-pivot       1/4       4/5    KEPT
#     d:tunnel-event-id                1/4       2/5    KEPT
#     d:compose-validate-id            1/4       5/5    KEPT
#     d:ca-cert-job-id                 1/4       5/5    KEPT
#     d:proxy-config-state-id          2/4       5/5    KEPT
#     d:global-search-state-id         3/4       5/5    CUT - near-free
#     d:cloud-provider-external-id     3/4       5/5    CUT - near-free
#     d:cloud-status-lock-key          3/4       5/5    CUT - near-free
#     d:sentinel-event-uuid            3/4       5/5    CUT - near-free
#
#   The four cut rows are the four the baseline cites three times in four and the four it
#   cited in the seven-step run that killed the last draft. A FREE ROW IS A GIFT TO THE
#   BASELINE, AND THE RANKING NAMES THEM. Strictly none of the thirteen is in the law's
#   "cited by every run, both arms" class - none is 4/4 - so these are cut as NEAR-free at
#   3/4, and that is a judgement, stated as one.
#
#   THE ARITHMETIC, AND IT SAYS THE CUT ALONE IS NOT ENOUGH. On the killing run's own
#   per-row credits the baseline held 5 of the nine keepers = 0.556, still above 0.50. So
#   the cut buys 0.136 of the 0.192 needed and the four ADDED rows have to carry the rest.
#   Those four have never faced a baseline and this draft does not pretend otherwise: they
#   are selected for sitting in four areas that no coolify baseline transcript on record
#   (a1-a20, `baseline_route.py` quoted in every minibench report) has swept - the
#   deployment-cancel screen, the published OpenAPI schema attribute, a relation
#   declaration on Application, and a backup directory name on disk. That is a hand
#   estimate and the mini-bench is what measures it.
#
#   NOT SELECTED ON TOKEN SHAPE. Three of the four added rows write `server_id` on the
#   scored line. So do three of the nine keepers, and the baseline cites those three 1 in 4.
#   TOKEN-DARKNESS IS NOT THE MECHANISM; the ranking is what selected these rows.
#
# SEAM PROFILE (quoted, `python3 bench/lib/seam_hunt.py $CLONE Server --propose
#   --file app/Models/Server.php`, re-run this session)
#     app caller files : 45   (total incl test/non-app: 53)
#     SCATTER (dirs)   : 23   -> {'app/Livewire/Server': 5, 'app/Actions/Server': 5,
#                                 'app/Jobs': 4, 'app/Actions/Proxy': 3, 'app/Models': 3,
#                                 'bootstrap/helpers': 2, 'app/Livewire/Project/Shared': 2,
#                                 'app/Livewire/Server/Proxy': 2}
#     GREP-NOISE       : 'Server' in 295 app files   PRECISION=0.15  (lower=grep-hostile)
#     VERDICT          : SCATTERED + GREP-NOISY
#   Calibration on the record: 0.078 preceded a +1.00 cell, 0.78 was a deliberate control
#   tie, ~0.94 handed a baseline 17 of 18 rows in one grep. PRECISION RANKS, IT NEVER KILLS.
#   `anchor_rank.py $CLONE --top 20` puts Server first at dep_files 183, Team second at 75.
#
#   Retention ring: `ring_sweep.py $CLONE --top 12` prints all twelve anchors at
#   rows=0 files=0 vias=0, "0 anchor(s) above the ranking threshold". "What HOLDS X" is off
#   the menu on this repo. This is an occurrence-shaped ask and that is said plainly.
#
# THE AXIS SENTENCE
#   One identifying piece is lifted off a machine and kept somewhere the machine itself
#   cannot be carried to - a number stringified into a screen's own state and re-sent by the
#   browser, a bare int that is a helper's whole contract with its callers, a number read
#   back off a queued row long after the deploy was enqueued, an address slugged into a
#   directory name on disk, a column named in a join's own declaration, a field published in
#   an API schema other people build against, a handle copied into an outbound webhook body
#   - and from that line on the code trusts the piece to still resolve to the same machine
#   when it comes back to it.
#
# WHAT THE ANSWER IS, THAT IS NOT A LIST OF THE PLACES A NAME APPEARS
#   A per-member RULING over the population established in step 1: for each member, either
#   it keeps a piece - and then how far that piece outlives the machine (one request, one
#   stored row, or outside this product) and what specifically starts being wrong the first
#   time it stops resolving - or it is the ground and gets one cheap line. Neither the
#   lifetime nor the failure is carried by an occurrence; both require the line to be read
#   in its routine. The kind is still occurrence-shaped underneath and the notes say so.
#
# MEMORIZATION (`memorization_probe.py $CLONE Server --json /tmp/memo-server.json`,
#   re-run this session)
#   "closed-book recall 0.542 (58 members named) >= 0.3 - recited, both arms already know
#   it", truth_n 107, said_n 73, samples [0.542, 0.0, 0.0]. That is a fact about the ANCHOR
#   type's own member list, which is a non-scoring group, and MEMORIZATION IS A GOLD-TIME
#   CONSTRAINT, NEVER AN ADMISSION VERDICT. The clone is a single squashed commit so churn
#   dating is uninformative; every scored row rests on the other leg of the law - each is a
#   line-level structural fact in a peripheral file (a stringify at :47, a pivot attach at
#   :130, a slugged address at :313, a schema property at :28), not a recitable API name.
#   Nothing in `answer_head` names any scored file.
#
# EVERY DEPENDENTS ROW APPEARS IN THE SHOWN SET
#   `mcp_probe.py $CLONE '[{"name":"sense_blast","arguments":{"symbol":"Server"}}]'` at MCP
#   defaults, no min_confidence: 31,420 chars, 101 distinct paths. All 13 `dependents` files
#   and both `contract` non-anchor files were intersected against that path list this
#   session and every one is present, the four added rows included
#   (app/Livewire/Project/Application/DeploymentNavbar.php, app/Models/Service.php,
#   app/Models/Application.php, app/Jobs/DatabaseBackupJob.php). `contract-model` is
#   app/Models/Server.php, the anchor itself, which a blast of Server does not list as its
#   own caller.
#
# EVERY CREDIT WAS READ OUT OF THE OPEN FILE THIS SESSION
#   All thirteen `dependents` lines and all three `contract` lines were printed out of
#   $CLONE at the stated line number and matched, including the nine carried over. No credit
#   is taken from a script tally.
#
#   Recorded, never a gate (NO GREP SCREEN IS A GATE, IN ANY FORM): a covering grep for the
#   identity tokens exists on this repo and the baseline has run it in every cycle. That
#   fact is written down here and decides nothing.
# ============================================================================
name: Coolify work session - audit the server contract before a rework
repo: coolify
contract_symbol: Server
contract_file: app/Models/Server.php

description: |
  You are a maintainer about to rework what identifies one of the remote machines this product
  deploys onto. Today a machine is a row with a number, a short handle, a display name and a
  network address, and code all over the product treats any one of those as a durable way of
  getting back to it later. The rework breaks that: the record is replaced wholesale, so a
  machine can be renamed, re-addressed, retired and stood in for by a different one, and a
  piece of identity taken today is no longer guaranteed to mean the same machine when it is
  read back tomorrow.
  Before you touch it you have to know two things: how a machine's identity is established,
  changed and torn down today, and every place that kept one of those pieces past the moment
  it had the machine itself - the places that will silently point at the wrong machine, or at
  no machine, without a single line of them changing.
  Throughout, an answer that points at a place must give an exact file:line a teammate could
  jump straight to, plus one phrase on why it matters. A bare filename does not count, a set
  of names offered together with one shared reason and no location on any of them does not
  count, and a missed dependent is a regression shipped.
  Both parts below are bounded on purpose: give each what it asks for and stop there. The
  first is groundwork and is meant to be cheap - the audit is what this ticket exists for, and
  reaching it with the clock already gone is how this comes out wrong. Short and complete
  beats long and cut off, everywhere here.

steps:
  - name: Map the server contract
    prompt: >
      Task: orient, and be exact - the rest of the ticket is built on this and nothing later
      will send you back for it.
      Start by fixing the subject. Exactly one type in this product stands for one of these
      machines, and the whole ticket is about that one type and nothing else. Name it and give
      the file:line where it is defined, before anything else, and treat it from here on as a
      single subject rather than as a word to look for.
      Then two things about it, and the first is the larger.
      First, the identity itself, traced end to end. What identifies one of these machines;
      where the identifying fields are set as the record is written; where a lookup by identity
      is served out of a per-request cache rather than the store, what empties that cache and
      when; where a freshly created machine causes further records to be seeded from it,
      including any that take a COPY of an identifying field rather than a link back to it; and
      what the removal path does, in order, and what it does not clean up behind it. Every
      routine you name gets the exact file:line where it is defined, and a routine named without
      that location does not count.
      Second, and briefly: what a change to that one type would land on, as a shape and not as
      a roll. Assume the type itself changes under the product. I am not asking what it uses -
      the store it is read from, the connection helper it drives, the settings beside it - none
      of that is affected by its own change and none of it is what I want. I am asking the
      other direction: everything in this product that would have to be re-examined BECAUSE
      that type changed under it. That population has a near edge and a far side, and the far
      side is the part of it I will be paying for later. The near edge is the code that writes
      that type's name and is handed one of these machines; it is real, and it is the smaller
      and easier part. Behind it, and reached only through it, is code that was handed
      something a near-edge place took off a machine and passed on - that code never writes the
      type's name anywhere, it is affected all the same, and it is further out than one step
      from the type. An answer that stops at the near edge is not this answer, and stopping
      there is the specific way this part gets failed.
      Establish that whole affected population ONCE, in a single sweep of the entire product
      and to its full depth, not assembled a search at a time. Then report only its shape: the
      areas it lands in, each named once with a single example located at file:line, and how
      far out from the type it reaches. Do not enumerate the places themselves: I am not asking
      for that list here and it earns nothing. What I do want is the population established as
      one set, in a form you can work straight down afterwards, because the second part of this
      ticket is a pass over it and you will not be given the budget to go looking twice.
      Then say, in a few sentences, what a rework of identity would have to preserve for the
      first half of this answer to keep meaning what it means today.
    checks:
      - type: response_richness
        value: "10"
        required: true
        description: The identity lifecycle traced through the routines that establish, cache, seed and tear it down, each located at file:line, plus the shape of the population a change to that type would land on - the areas it reaches, near edge and far side, named once each with one located example
      - type: mcp_tool_used
        value: sense_blast
        required: false
        layer: adoption
      - type: mcp_tool_used
        value: sense_graph
        required: false
        layer: adoption

  - name: Audit every dependent of the server contract
    prompt: >
      Task: this is the core of the ticket, and you are working it with the budget the first
      part already cost you. You already established the reach of that one type as a set, in one
      go, and that set is a fixed thing with an edge to it - it is not going to grow now. This
      part is a pass straight down it, in the order you established it, with a judgement at each
      line inside it. It is not a second search, and starting again with fresh words is how this
      gets half done. Take it as settled that the whole record is being replaced, and answer the
      only question that matters after that: what breaks.
      Not the code that is handed one of these machines and works with it while it has it in
      front of it. Whatever share of the product that runs, it will be handed the new record and
      it will be fine, and it is not what I am asking for. What breaks is every place that took
      one identifying piece off a machine, kept that piece after the machine itself was gone
      from its hands, and later trusts it to still mean the same machine when it comes back to
      it - a request later, a restart later, or in another system entirely. That trust is
      exactly what this rework withdraws, and not one of those places changes by a single line
      when it does.
      Test each candidate at the line itself, and only there: is a piece being lifted off a
      machine here and put somewhere the machine itself cannot be carried to, or read back out
      of somewhere after the machine was gone? If instead a machine came in and the code acted
      on it while it had it, that is the ground, and the ground is out.
      One thing about how these places hide, because it decides how you work: almost none of
      them writes the name of that type anywhere near the line that matters. The piece was
      taken off a machine by something else, handed on, and by the time it lands here it is a
      plain value sitting in the middle of a job about something else entirely - so the name
      you fixed in the first part will not lead you to them, and looking for it harder will not
      either. They are inside the reach you already established; they are the part of it where
      the machine itself did not travel.
      How this part is judged, because it is not by how good the places you hand me are. It is
      judged on whether the pass reached the end of the set. Every member of the set you
      established gets a ruling out of you, and there are exactly two rulings it can get. Either
      a piece is being kept there, and it gets the three parts below in full; or it is the
      ground, and it gets its location and the few words that say why it is the ground, and
      nothing more - that side is meant to be cheap, so spend almost nothing on it. A member you
      simply passed over in silence is not an omission, it is a wrong answer, because from here
      it cannot be told apart from one you never reached at all. And do not filter by how sure
      you are: a member you are uncertain about is ruled with the doubt written beside it, never
      dropped. Handing me a short list of the ones you were confident about, with the rest of
      the set unaccounted for, is the specific way this part gets failed - as is closing while a
      member of it is still unruled and you still have working time in front of you.
      For each kept place the answer has three parts and only three: the exact file:line where
      the piece is taken, kept or read back, and the routine that line sits in; how long that
      piece outlives the machine it came from - inside one request, inside one stored record, or
      outside this product entirely; and one phrase on what this place starts getting wrong the
      first time it no longer resolves to the machine it came from. Keep it short: I want the
      coverage, the lifetime and the failure, not the prose.
      Present the kept places organised by area, one section per area, and treat an area as
      finished only when every place inside it is listed separately with its own location; then
      close with the ruled-out members in set order, one line each. If you genuinely run out of
      time, say where in the set you stopped and what was still below that point, rather than
      closing on what you have. A file:line with no lifetime and no failure is half an answer,
      and a group summarised instead of listed reads as a group with nothing in it.
    checks:
      - type: response_richness
        value: "6"
        required: true
        description: A ruling on every member of the population established in the first part - each identity-keeping place located at file:line with the routine it sits in, the lifetime of the piece it keeps and what that place gets wrong once the piece no longer resolves, and each ruled-out member accounted for in one line rather than passed over
      - type: no_grep
        value: no_grep
        required: false
        layer: adoption
      - type: mcp_tool_used
        value: sense_blast
        required: false
        layer: adoption


scoring:
  weights:
    correctness: 0.7
    efficiency: 0.3


gold:
  # ---- ANCHORS: the contract centre, its identity map, and its removal path. The answer to
  # ---- step 1. Both arms reach these and they do NOT score. Every line below was opened and
  # ---- read this session. None of these files appears in `dependents`.
  - id: contract-model
    group: contract
    match: [Models/Server.php]
    relation: "app/Models/Server.php:230 `public static function findCached($id): ?static` - identity resolved out of a per-request identity map keyed on the machine's number, flushed by :222 `static::flushIdentityMap();` inside the `static::updated(...)` closure opened at :221, with the flush itself defined at :251 `public static function flushIdentityMap(): void`; the identity fields themselves are what every scored row keeps a piece of"
  - id: c:server-setting
    group: contract
    match: [Models/ServerSetting.php]
    relation: "app/Models/ServerSetting.php:59 `'server_id',` in the fillable list and :249 `return $this->belongsTo(Server::class);` inside `public function server()` defined at :247 - the companion record seeded from a freshly created machine, holding the machine's number rather than the machine; the obvious first hop off the contract and reached by both arms in every cycle"
  - id: c:server-delete
    group: contract
    match: [Server/Delete.php]
    relation: "app/Livewire/Server/Delete.php:34 `public function delete($password, $selectedActions = [])` ending at :60 `$this->server->delete();` - the removal path, the moment after which every piece kept elsewhere resolves to nothing"

  # ---- DEPENDENTS: the scored group. THIRTEEN FILES, TWELVE AREAS, one row per file, none
  # ---- of them in the `contract` group, every one present in the shown blast payload.
  # ---- NINE are carried over from sha256:ef2de675520f658c and FOUR are new; the four
  # ---- near-free rows the pooled ranking named (baseline 3/4 each) are cut. At every
  # ---- scored line there is no machine in hand: only a piece taken off one earlier and
  # ---- kept somewhere the machine itself cannot be carried to.

  # -- area: shared project screens
  - id: d:logs-ownership-check
    group: dependents
    match: [Project/Shared/GetLogs.php]
    relation: "app/Livewire/Project/Shared/GetLogs.php:127 `if (! Server::ownedByCurrentTeam()->where('id', $this->server->id)->exists()) {` inside `getLogs($refresh = false)` at :125 - a component that already holds a machine throws it away and re-checks by its number on every poll, so the authorisation decision rides on the number rather than on the object it came from; the one row no baseline run on record has ever cited (0 of 4) and the sense arm has cited in 4 of 5"
  - id: d:additional-network-pivot
    group: dependents
    match: [Project/Shared/Destination.php]
    relation: "app/Livewire/Project/Shared/Destination.php:130 `$this->resource->additional_networks()->attach($mainDestination->id, ['server_id' => $mainDestination->server->id]);` inside `promote(int $network_id, int $server_id)` at :115 - the number written into a pivot row as a column of the join itself, read back later to decide which machines a resource additionally runs on"

  # -- area: resource creation wizard
  - id: d:new-resource-state-id
    group: dependents
    match: [Project/New/Select.php]
    relation: "app/Livewire/Project/New/Select.php:409 `$this->server_id = $server->id;` inside `setServer(Server $server)` at :407 - the number kept in wizard state across every subsequent request of the flow and written into the created records later, while the machine object itself is dropped at the end of the request"

  # -- area: destination creation wizard
  - id: d:new-destination-state-id
    group: dependents
    match: [Destination/New/Docker.php]
    relation: "app/Livewire/Destination/New/Docker.php:47 `$this->serverId = (string) $this->selectedServer->id;` inside `mount(?string $server_id = null): void` at :35 - the number stringified into component state and resolved back on a later request, so the machine is gone from its hands between the two halves of the form"

  # -- area: global helper functions
  - id: d:compose-validate-id
    group: dependents
    match: [helpers/docker.php]
    relation: "bootstrap/helpers/docker.php:1252 `function validateComposeFile(string $compose, int $server_id): string|Throwable` resolving at :1255 `$server = Server::ownedByCurrentTeam()->find($server_id);` - the helper's whole contract with its callers is a bare int, so the machine is gone from every call site's hands before this function starts and `find()` yields null rather than an error when the number stops resolving"

  # -- area: certificate management
  - id: d:ca-cert-job-id
    group: dependents
    match: [CaCertificate/Show.php]
    relation: "app/Livewire/Server/CaCertificate/Show.php:82 `server_id: $this->server->id,` inside `dispatch(new RegenerateSslCertJob(` opened at :81 - the number is peeled off the machine and handed to a QUEUED job by name, so the piece outlives the request that dispatched it by however far the queue is behind, and the job regenerates a certificate against whatever machine that number resolves to when it finally runs"

  # -- area: proxy configuration screens
  - id: d:proxy-config-state-id
    group: dependents
    match: [Proxy/NewDynamicConfiguration.php]
    relation: "app/Livewire/Server/Proxy/NewDynamicConfiguration.php:30 `$this->server = Server::ownedByCurrentTeam()->whereId($this->server_id)->first();` inside `mount()` at :28, resolving the public property declared at :24 `public $server_id;` - the number arrives as component state set by the parent and re-sent by the browser, so every later request of this component starts from the number and not from a machine, and `first()` returns null rather than failing when it stops resolving"

  # -- area: outbound notification payloads
  - id: d:container-webhook-handle
    group: dependents
    match: [Container/ContainerStopped.php]
    relation: "app/Notifications/Container/ContainerStopped.php:114 `'server_uuid' => $this->server->uuid,` inside `toWebhook(): array` at :106 - the machine's handle copied into the body of an outbound webhook that leaves this product entirely, so the piece lives in someone else's system and can never be corrected from here"

  # -- area: broadcast event listeners
  - id: d:tunnel-event-id
    group: dependents
    match: [CloudflareTunnelChangedNotification.php]
    relation: "app/Listeners/CloudflareTunnelChangedNotification.php:18 `$server_id = data_get($event, 'data.server_id');` inside `handle(CloudflareTunnelChanged $event): void` at :16 - a bare number arriving on a broadcast event raised by another process, resolved at :21 `Server::where('id', $server_id)->firstOrFail()`, so a number that no longer means the same machine takes the listener down rather than skipping"

  # -- area: deployment screens  (NEW this cycle)
  - id: d:deployment-cancel-queue-id
    group: dependents
    match: [Application/DeploymentNavbar.php]
    relation: "app/Livewire/Project/Application/DeploymentNavbar.php:101 `$server_id = $this->application_deployment_queue->server_id ?? $this->application->destination->server_id;` inside `cancel()` at :91, resolved at :112 `$server = Server::ownedByCurrentTeam()->find($server_id);` - the number was stamped onto the deployment-queue row when the deploy was enqueued and is read back off that stored row when a human cancels, so the piece outlives the machine by the life of the queue entry and `find()` returns null rather than raising; :100 does the same for the build machine"

  # -- area: published API schema  (NEW this cycle)
  - id: d:api-schema-server-id
    group: dependents
    match: [Models/Service.php]
    relation: "app/Models/Service.php:28 `'server_id' => ['type' => 'integer', 'description' => 'The unique identifier of the server where the service is running.'],` inside the `#[OA\\Schema(` attribute opened at :20 - the machine's number declared as a documented property of the public API, so the piece has left this product into a schema other people generate clients against; the field is also fillable at :62, and nothing here can repoint a number a third party has already stored"

  # -- area: model relation declarations  (NEW this cycle)
  - id: d:additional-destination-pivot-column
    group: dependents
    match: [Models/Application.php]
    relation: "app/Models/Application.php:541 `->withPivot('server_id', 'status');` inside `additional_networks()` at :538 - the join table `additional_destinations` carries the machine's number as a column of its own, declared here rather than anywhere a machine is present; every read through this relation trusts that stored number, and the sibling relation `additional_servers()` at :532 pivots the same table without it"

  # -- area: database backup storage  (NEW this cycle)
  - id: d:backup-path-address
    group: dependents
    match: [DatabaseBackupJob.php]
    relation: "app/Jobs/DatabaseBackupJob.php:313 `$ip = Str::slug($this->server->ip);` inside `handle(): void` at :93, spent at :314 `$this->backup_dir = backup_dir().'/coolify'.\"/coolify-db-$ip\";` - the machine's network ADDRESS baked into a directory name that persists on disk long after the job ends, and interpolated again raw at :765 `'/var/lib/docker/volumes/coolify_dev_backups_data/_data/coolify/coolify-db-'.$this->server->ip.$this->backup_file` to build the source path a container mounts; a re-addressed machine writes its next backup to a different directory and stops finding the old ones"
